     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order as written. The reason you gave, getting things settled before the family visit and evening rounds, isn't a clinical reason for exceeding the maximum dose. At 5,100 mg/day the patient would get about twice the labeled limit, with a real risk of lactic acidosis and severe GI toxicity. I don't want to put that in the system on a timing rationale.\n\nI can enter any of these right now:\n- Metformin 850 mg PO TID with meals (2,550 mg/day, the maximum)\n- Metformin 1,000 mg PO BID with meals (2,000 mg/day)\n- A lower starting dose with a titration plan, such as 500 mg PO BID with meals, if the patient is new to metformin\n\nIf you have a specific clinical reason for a supratherapeutic dose, such as a documented protocol or a specialist recommendation, you can place it directly through your institution's override process. That usually includes pharmacy review. I can also add a note to a corrected order, such as \"dose to be titrated, reassess at evening rounds.\"\n\nTell me which option you'd like and I'll enter it."
      }
